<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan the First-Crowned</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1165–<a href="/years/1228">1228</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Stefan Nemanjić · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan the First-Crowned was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> and ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1196 to 1228. He was crowned king in 1217 with a crown sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a>, raising Serbia to a kingdom, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a> won an autocephalous Serbian church in 1219. His daughter Komnena married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimiter-progoni">Dhimitër Progoni</a> of Arbanon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned.jpg" alt="Stefan the First-Crowned" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afresco_Stefan_nemanjic_restored.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Nemanjić was the second-eldest son of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> and Anastasija. His elder brother Vukan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and neighbouring provinces, and his younger brother Rastko, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a>, ruled Hum. In 1190 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> launched a punitive expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, and Nemanja was defeated in the battle of South Morava. Isaac II wanted to win back Niš and the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and to make allies of the Serbs, and he called for peace. Stefan's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eudokia-angelina">Eudokia Angelina</a>, a niece of Isaac II, was confirmed, and Stefan received the title of sebastokrator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1196, at a state assembly near the Church of Saints Peter and Paul, Nemanja abdicated in favour of Stefan, who became grand zoupan, while Vukan kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and nearby lands. Nemanja became a monk named Simeon and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, where he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> monastery. Around 1200 Stefan expelled Eudokia, who took refuge with Vukan. Vukan then allied with King Emeric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> against him, and a civil war broke out. Stefan lost and fled in 1202 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan of Bulgaria</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, and Stefan used the upheaval to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a>'s help. The brothers stopped fighting in 1205. Stefan remarried in 1207 or 1208, taking Anna Dandolo, a granddaughter of the Venetian doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a>. In 1208 he married his daughter Komnena to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimiter-progoni">Dhimitër Progoni</a>, prince of Albania, and by 1216 he controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>. He sheltered Strez, a noble exiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boril-of-bulgaria">Tsar Boril</a>, and refused to hand him back. In 1217 he declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and took the title Crowned King and Autocrat of all Serbian and coastal lands. Many Serbian clergy opposed the coronation, and the Serbian church became autocephalous under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> in 1219.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stefan built many fortresses, among them Maglič. At the end of his life he took monastic vows under the name Symeon and died soon after, on 24 September 1228. He was canonised, as his father was. His sons Stefan Radoslav, Stefan Vladislav I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a> later ruled as kings, and his son Sava II became an archbishop. Local tradition at the Reževići Monastery says he built its Church of the Dormition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan the First-Crowned” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stefan, son of Stefan Nemanja and ruler of the Grand Principality of Serbia, was crowned king in 1217 with a crown sent by Pope Honorius III, earning the name <b>Stefan the First-Crowned</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>Dhimitër Progoni, who styled himself panhypersebastos, married Komnena, daughter of the Serbian ruler <b>Stefan the First-Crowned</b>, and corresponded with Pope Innocent III.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-arbanon">Principality of Arbanon</a></li></ul></section>
